# Active Record Patterns

This project does **not** use fat `ApplicationRecord` domain models or `app/services/`.

Persistence lives in flattened Active Record classes at `app/models/` (`User`, `UserSession`, `UserEmailConfirmation`, `UserPasswordReset`). Domain orchestration lives in `ApplicationForm` subclasses under `app/models/<context>/` (e.g. `User::Registration`). See `AGENTS.md` for the full layer map.

Do not introduce `*::Record`, mappers, entities, repository interfaces, or adapters. Do not nest table models (`User::Session`).

## Records

```ruby
class User < ApplicationRecord
  has_many :sessions, class_name: "UserSession", dependent: :destroy
  has_many :email_confirmations, class_name: "UserEmailConfirmation", dependent: :destroy
  has_many :password_resets, class_name: "UserPasswordReset", dependent: :destroy

  has_secure_password

  generates_token_for :email_confirmation, expires_in: User::Email::CONFIRMATION_TOKEN_EXPIRES_IN do
    [ email, verified ]
  end
end

class UserSession < ApplicationRecord
  belongs_to :user

  scope :active, -> { where(refresh_token_expires_at: Time.current..) }

  def self.revoke_all
    update_all(refresh_token_expires_at: 1.second.ago)
  end
end
```

Rules:

- Name persistence classes after the table (`User`, `UserSession`, `UserEmailConfirmation`), not nested namespaces (`User::Session`) and not `*::Record`.
- Place table models at `app/models/` (`user.rb`, `user_session.rb`, `user_email_confirmation.rb`). Nested paths under `app/models/<context>/` are forms, domain modules, or serializers.
- On the parent, declare `class_name:` when the association name does not match the class.
- Always declare `dependent:` on associations that own child records.
- Keep Records thin: associations, `has_secure_password`, `generates_token_for`, scopes, and small persistence helpers (`revoke_all`).
- Do **not** put use-case orchestration, mail delivery, or multi-step workflows in Records — use an `ApplicationForm`.

## Associations & queries

- Use scopes on Records for simple, reusable filters (`active`, `pending`, `not_expired`).
- Prevent N+1 with `includes` / `preload` / `eager_load` when reading associations.
- Use `joins` when filtering without loading associated records.
- Prefer `find_each` for large batches.

```ruby
# Bad — N+1
User.all.each { |user| user.sessions.count }

# Good
User.includes(:sessions)
```

## Callbacks

Avoid callbacks for business logic and side effects.

```ruby
# Avoid — side effect buried in the Record
class User < ApplicationRecord
  after_create :send_welcome_email
end

# Prefer — explicit orchestration in a form (see User::Registration)
```

## Migrations

```ruby
class CreateUsers < ActiveRecord::Migration[8.1]
  def change
    create_table :users, id: :uuid do |t|
      t.string :first_name, null: false
      t.string :last_name, null: false
      t.string :email, null: false, index: { unique: true }
      t.string :password_digest, null: false
      t.boolean :verified, null: false, default: false
      t.boolean :active, null: false, default: true

      t.timestamps
    end
  end
end
```

- Prefer UUID primary keys (`id: :uuid`). The PostgreSQL adapter default is `uuidv7()` from `config/initializers/uuid_v7_default.rb`. `pgcrypto` is enabled for the database.
- Add `null:` and sensible `default:` constraints at the DB level.
- Add indexes for columns used in `where` / `order` / joins and unique business keys.
- In data migrations, prefer `find_each`; `update_column(s)` skips validations, callbacks, and timestamps — use deliberately.

## Concerns

Use only for genuinely shared, cohesive persistence behavior — not as a dumping ground for domain logic.

## Performance

- Add DB indexes for frequently queried columns.
- Use `select` to limit columns; `pluck` instead of `map` for single attributes.
- Use `find_each` for large datasets.
